  data:  # 31a9
    kind: work_item  # 31a9
    title: "Go: close the package-stripped resolution-order hijack BEFORE carrying initializer types into the module slot (17.1% wrong-symbol rate measured)"  # 31a9
    status: todo_hard  # 31a9
    priority: 3  # 31a9
    parent:  # 31a9
    tags:  # 31a9
    - analysis_quality  # 31a9
    - io_boundary  # 31a9
    - taint  # 31a9
    isbefore: []  # 31a9
    duplicate_of: []  # 31a9
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 31a9
    pr_ref:  # 31a9
    description: "Go's receiver typing is HEALTHY; its residual gap is initializer type INFERENCE.\nThe obvious fix for it is NET-HARMFUL and must not be built as-is.\n\nFiled from the WI-zilag measurement sweep, where Go was measured as the\nsecond-language control. The valuable result is the negative one.\n\n=== GO DOES NOT HAVE PYTHON'S ANNOTATED-RECEIVER GAP ===\n\nMeasured, production analyzer, 5 Go trees: 3,618 / 3,741 = 96.7% of\npackage-qualified-receiver catalogue-name call sites already reach a usable\nmodule slot, and 430 annotated-PARAMETER sites are io_boundary-tagged by\nproduction today. Positive control:\n\n    func h(c net.Conn, b []byte) { c.Write(b) }\n        -> go:net:0-0:Write:unresolved -> net.Conn.Write / net_send\n\nSo WI-zilag's Python producer fix has NO Go counterpart to build, and a single\nshared producer mechanism across the two languages is the WRONG factoring.\n\n=== THE ACTUAL GO GAP ===\n\nInitializer type inference drops the package qualifier:\n\n    tr := &http.Transport{}       // type not carried to the module slot\n    bw := bufio.NewWriter(w)      // same\n\n=== WHY THE OBVIOUS FIX IS NET-HARMFUL (measured, do not skip this) ===\n\nA candidate producer fix that carries the initializer's type into the module\nslot was A/B'd in-process on 5 Go trees with the repo untouched:\n\n  - 118 of 692 moved edges (17.1%) point at the WRONG IN-REPO SYMBOL, because\n    Go strips the package prefix and probes the repo symbol table BEFORE the\n    import-path fallback. 66 of those 118 (55.9%) land on a struct `:field`.\n    Per repo: alertmanager 38/89, caddy and others similar.\n  - User-facing gain, cold-cache two-arm CLI A/B on `total_io_edges`:\n    caddy 228->232 (+4), kserve 253->257 (+4), alertmanager 211->211 (+0),\n    prometheus 617->617 (+0). Total +8 on a 1,309 baseline = +0.61%.\n    dnscrypt-proxy (2.5% of the corpus) not measured.\n\n17.1% hijack rate for +0.61% recall is the wrong trade for a security tool.\n\n=== DISPUTED, AND RECORDED AS DISPUTED ===\n\nOne measurement lens claimed closing this gap makes INV-fazim strictly BETTER\n(-13.6% fazim population, -406 taint sink matches, zero gains on the ungated\nhits[0] channel, +25 io tagged edges with zero losses). An adversarial verifier\nmeasured the hijacks above and inverted that verdict. Both ran production code.\nThe disagreement is NOT resolved and must be resolved before any Go work here:\nthe two are not obviously incompatible (removals on the ungated channel and\nhijacks on the resolution channel can coexist), so the open question is the NET\neffect on findings, which nobody measured.\n\n=== WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS BUILDABLE ===\n\nFix the resolution ORDER first: Go must consult the import-path fallback BEFORE\nprobing the repo symbol table for a package-stripped name. That is the defect\nproducing the 17.1% hijack rate, it is independent of initializer inference, and\nit is plausibly the same root cause as INV-fazim (go marking an external\ninstance-method call is_resolved=True on an `external` dst). Size that first.\n\nDO NOT carry initializer types into the module slot until the hijack channel is\nclosed and the disagreement above is resolved with a finding-level measurement."  # 31a9
    fields: {}  # 31a9
- op: discuss  # cde5
  at: '2026-08-25T20:11:46Z'  # cde5
  by: agent  # cde5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # cde5
  clock: 2  # cde5
  nonce: cde5  # cde5
  message: "LIVE CORROBORATION OF THE HIJACK CHANNEL, from measurement 0005 (docs/measurements/0005-taint-precision-after-vocabulary-split.md), with a user-visible consequence this item had not yet recorded.\n\nWHAT WAS SEEN. In caddy, the taint walk reports a call-graph route containing the hop `modules/caddytls/capools.go StoragePool.Provision -> caddy.go Load`. That hop does not exist. capools.go:358 calls `ca.storage.Load(ctx, caID)` -- an INTERFACE METHOD on certmagic.Storage -- and it was resolved to `caddy.Load(cfgJSON []byte, forceReload bool)`, the package-level function in the repo's own root package. That is exactly the failure this item names: Go strips the package prefix and probes the repo symbol table BEFORE the import-path fallback, so a bare `Load` finds the wrong in-repo symbol. It is not the initializer-inference gap; it is the resolution-ORDER defect that this item says must be fixed first.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE 17.1% RATE ALREADY ON RECORD. The hijack does not merely mis-attribute one edge -- it FABRICATES A ROUTE THROUGH THE CONFIG PIPELINE, and every taint flow that walks through it inherits a path a reader cannot follow. In 0005 the same fictitious shape appeared under FOUR separate situations (untrusted-input-no-host-fs 2/3/5/6 and host-description-no-network 2/3 route through a sibling fabrication, `internal/filesystems/map.go FileSystemMap.Get -> caddy.Load`, where FileSystemMap.Get is a plain sync.Map lookup that calls nothing). Two independent adjudicators found the fabrication on their own and said so unprompted.\n\nTHE SHARPEST FORM OF THE HARM, because it cuts both ways. For caddy:untrusted-input-no-host-fs:2 the reported route is fictitious AND the flow is a genuine true positive -- the real chain runs UP out of the source symbol through cmdRun to caddy.Load, a direction the tool never claimed. So the hijack does not simply add false positives: it makes a CORRECT finding uncheckable from its own record. Under the 0001 rubric that flow is rescued only by the \"fictitious route, real chain\" clause; judged on the route the tool actually printed, the hop is real dynamic dispatch and the finding would be UNADJUDICABLE.\n\nNO NEW MEASUREMENT IS CLAIMED HERE and the 17.1%/+0.61% trade on this item is not re-derived. This is corroboration that the hijack channel is live on a current tree at a repo already in this item's own A/B corpus, plus a consequence (route fabrication feeding the taint walk) to weigh when the disputed net-effect question is finally measured."  # cde5
